    {
      "id": "1",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1,
        "relationship_type": "synonym",
        "body": "Both がある and がいる combine the particle が with a verb to express existence or possession. The difference is that ある refers to inanimate objects or intangible concepts (e.g., 車がある = 'I have a car' or 'There is a car,' アイデアがある = 'I have an idea'), while いる is used for living or animate things (e.g., 猫がいる = 'I have a cat' or 'There is a cat').",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 13,
          "slug": "がある",
          "title": "がある",
          "meaning": "To be, There is",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 23,
          "slug": "がいる",
          "title": "がいる",
          "meaning": "To be, There is,  To remain (in a state)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "809",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 809,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "がいる combines the particle が with the verb いる to express the existence of living or animate beings, like people or animals. いらっしゃる is the honorific equivalent of いる, as well as 行く and 来る. In other words, がいらっしゃる is the honorific version of がいる, and いらっしゃいますか is the honorific equivalent of いますか, 行きますか, or 来ますか.",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 191,
          "slug": "いらっしゃる",
          "title": "いらっしゃる",
          "meaning": "To be (Honorific), To come (Honorific), To go (Honorific)",
          "level": "JLPT4",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
